No. Raising the pool size would only delay the next exhaustion, because `withClient` in `db.js` leaks connections:

```js
const result = await fn(client);
client.release();
```

If `fn` throws, `client.release()` never runs and that connection stays checked out for good. Each failed query permanently removes one of the 20 slots. `pool.log` matches that pattern: `idle` is always 0 and `waiting` keeps climbing (7, 11, 14, 19, 23, 28) until requests hit the 2000ms `connectionTimeoutMillis`. A pool that was simply too small under load would show `idle` rising again between bursts.

To fix it, release the client in a `finally` block so it goes back to the pool whether or not `fn` throws:

```js
async function withClient(fn) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    return await fn(client);
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

Also check whether any code calls `pool.connect()` directly instead of going through `withClient`, since those call sites could leak the same way.

